    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "No problem. I'm glad to be here. I hope y'all can hear me. Yep, can hear you great. So my name is Yolanda Gordon and I am coming to you from South Carolina. I just wanted to share a little bit about myself and to just tell you why SNAP is so important. SNAP benefits and safety net programs were instrumental to my ability to graduate from college with a degree and to be able to feed my children. The threats to SNAP are crushing blow to 2 million Americans. And with 19 billion in cuts over 10 years and work requirements and work training requirements, it will make it more difficult for many Americans to get the access to food that they need. So there are several things that we can do. We need to continue to engage those in Congress to talk to people that are affected by these cuts. We also need to get tough on politicians that tell family values that while they're sitting and thinking of cutting these necessary benefits that they are affecting families negatively and that they must protect those that would be decimated by their decisions. And we need to remind Congress that the cost of living is rising and wages are stagnant and families are suffering. And that there are many people like myself who are the return on investment for SNAP benefits that I don't feel that my voice gets acknowledged as much. And we need to remind them to seek out voices like ours that have experienced what SNAP benefits and that the risk of these cuts far outweighs the benefits that they may see and that Special interest groups and think tanks don't need to make those decisions. We need to be the people that speak to them that they listen to. And after just passing a $1.5 trillion tax cut for the wealthy, I think that they could protect individuals that need these benefits.",
