    "turn_text": "going to cut the program by more than $23 billion. It really is pretty extraordinary. You know, we are a nation of plenty. We have an abundance of food. What we ought to do is to work to eradicate hunger and not eradicate the anti-hunger programs. And so often, there are so many families who don't know where their next meal is coming from. I did an event today in Hampton, Connecticut. And one of the things that the United Way has partnered with the AmeriCorps VISTA program is to work with kids in the Hampton school system to distribute food to those who are most needy. Now they're not just doing that because they think it's a problem. They've examined this, they know it's a problem. So they are pulling themselves together to use and to work with our school kids and the AmeriCorps and VISTA volunteers to get food distributed to the neediest people. You know, people talk about food insecurity. I don't buy that phrase. It's not people who are hungry. In the third congressional district, which I represent, one in seven people do not know where their next meal is coming from. And the food stand program is essential. It helps to keep people from going hungry. It's helped more than 44 million Americans, 2016, including nearly 35,000 families in Connecticut's third district. So we know the program works for people who need it most. It works for households with kids, and in average months, food stamps help families with nearly 20 million children afford an adequate diet. One in four kids in the United States. That's one in four kids. In Connecticut, one out of eight residents rely on the food stamp program for access to healthy meals, more than half of Connecticut's staff, snap recipients are families with kids. We know about its economic benefits for every dollar, In benefits over 91 cents goes directly to the households with incomes at or below the poverty line. One in half of the benefits go to households in the deepest poverty and over 70% of all benefits go to households with kids. So let me just tell you, in my view, the Republicans were serious about reforming the farm bill. What they would have done is include the limit on the agricultural",
