    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "What we need to do is if they were serious, to deal with limiting the agricultural subsidies that they provide for crop insurance, That is a federal program that has no eligibility, taps or no payment limits. So, you know, it marks you the attacks on the SNAP program attacks on dignity. And it's about the way Paul Ryan and the President think about poverty. If you can only make receiving government aid onerous enough and humiliating enough, then people will opt out voluntarily and they will redouble their efforts to avoid hunger illness or being laid off. This is a richest nation on the earth. We, people in this country should not be going hungry. And you know, just one other point for you, you know, in the past, the food stamp program enjoyed bipartisan support. That was true for decades, because the leaders recognized a moral obligation to combat hunger in this country. But instead, we have President Trump, we have Paul Ryan, who rushed through a $2 trillion tax cut. The benefits go mostly to the wealthiest in the corporations. So I want to just say thank you to all of you for being active, for drawing attention to the importance of defending the COSNAM program. And there's nothing more powerful than people hearing directly from folks who are in this struggle. I tell you over, I say this over and over again, Congress is an institution that responds to external pressure. So you all have to make as much noise as you can and just say enough is enough. We got to stand together to protect our neediest families and our kids. So thank you for allowing me to join your call and for commenting on the fight. We're in it. We're in it together. And we're not going to allow them to do this. And we cannot allow them to do it. So I thank you for listening to me. And I'm happy to try to answer any questions if that's appropriate.",
