  {
    "episode_id": 75093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "am. Thanks, Meredith, and good evening, everyone. I'm really glad to be with all you tonight and to be talking together about our May action focused on making sure Congress says no to this proposal through our work in the media. And, you know, sometimes people will ask me, you know, why bother with local media? Why focus on it? What differences really make who's paying attention to it? And just a couple of months ago, I wanted to share this story. I was talking to the opinions editor at a local paper in the Midwest. And we're talking about what a challenging time this is for local media. And he said, you know, Colin, we've lost some subscribers over the years. But the one audience I know we still have are members of Congress. If we write something critical of the member here at the paper, I know that the first voicemail waiting for me when I get to the office in the morning will be from congressional staff. And that's because congressional staff every morning are furiously pouring over the local paper. Because the truth is, they see that local paper as a lot of ways even more important to their work than the New York Times or CNN or Fox might be. Because they know that 100% of the readers of your local paper also are people who live in the district that those members of Congress represent, and are the people who vote them in office every two years. So, you know, this month, and we're about to get to some specifics, but this month, when you're sitting down to write, remember that the media is a tool that not only amplifies your message, but also really amplifies your influence on capitalism. Because as advocates, ultimately, our audience with the media is not the public, is not journalists, but is members of the Congress, communicating to them in this public way via the media. And as Meredith just explained, time is of the essence. Thinking about the potential vote that could be happening this month, we want to get our message into the media as soon as humanly possible. So we're focused in particular on letters the editor, those short, you know, 100 or 200 word opportunities to get our names in print. And we want to really blanket that map of the country over the next few weeks with hits, standing up for our message. And, you know, we heard from Representative Delora, and we heard from Yolanda, and we heard from Meredith of some specifics in this bill, and even Meredith was talking about, you know, the distinctions between total cuts and the net cuts. And, you know, I want to acknowledge that this is a confusing bill, the way that it undermines SNAP is complicated, you know, through, you know, in some cases, things that may sound like a good idea but aren't confusing rhetoric. seemingly benign changes that could actually do real harm. But the point of a letter to the editor is not about explaining every last detail of policy. You could spend 150 words just trying to explain what categorical eligibility is. These letters are not about that. They are about making sure Congress understands. Number one, why this issue matters, and number two, what we want them to do about it. So to that end, really thinking about before we sit down to write, what are the key messages we want Congress to hear? And that first one is that this farm bill will make it harder for people to put food on the table. And second, that for years, Congress has stayed united in a bipartisan way to make sure people have enough to eat, and that can't stop now, and they need to say no to this bill or any bill like it. But before we can actually get those messages to Congress, we first have to get them past our local editors for the ones who decide what actually gets published. So I wanted to take a minute to share just a couple of tips, and you see the shorthand for them up on the screen there. Some tips about how to make sure that the letters we write and send in actually make it into the paper and can be useful to our advocacy. And the first is make it personal. You'll see in a second, we have a template that we welcome you to use as a guide when you sit down to write your letter, work from it, use it, but form letters are not what move members of Congress to act. They really want to hear from their constituents, and that's you. You know, if you've ever used SNAP, share with the program and to you. If you're a teacher who sees the impact of hunger on your kids, you know, the example that Betsy just gave with the work that she does. you personally ever gone through a time when money was tight. You know, maybe you're someone who just gets a little crabby-inspired when you're hungry, right? Like, this is an issue that is really personal. It's about food. It's about access to food and nutrition for people in our communities and people in our country. And if it's a personal issue, our media needs to reflect that. So number one, make it personal. Number two, make it timely. And sorry, I'm realizing, sorry, number two is make it local, as I actually looked at my list here. Local papers cover local issues, right? Even just like quite literally mentioning the name of your town, in that letter can make a really big difference in making this national policy issue feel local. You know, instead of talking about how this bill will take throughout the table for people, talk about Minnesotans or Texans or Hoosers or whatever you call people from your state, and even better add in a little bit of local data. On the next slide in a second, you'll see links to fact sheets with data above the impact of SNAP. in your community and you can drop that right in. And then most importantly, mention your member of Congress by me. That both really makes it more local and more powerful on Capitol Hill. The third tip is to make it timely. You know that that sort of root word in news is new. Editors are looking for things that are new and that are timely. And the best way to do this is to reference a recent story that was in the paper in your letter. And you can see some examples of this on our website. But, you know, in some cases, you may actually be able to respond to a story about the farm bill because this has been making use. But there are lots of other hooks too. You know, is there a story about a local food bank? Is there a story about kids and teachers in the countdown to summer? Is there a story about gridlock in Congress? Was there a recent round of layoffs in your town? All of these work as hooks. And if you can't spot something in the paper already, and this isn't always the case for stuff we work on here at results, but in this particular instance, this issue itself is timely. Congress can be voting on this this month and say that in your letter let editors know that because it helps them realize the urgency of what you're trying to communicate and why they need to publish it. And, you know, finally, if you zoned out or started checking your phone or already forget what I just said, that's fine. You can ignore all of that if you remember this, that the single most important strategy for getting letters published. is to actually send them in. You know, for every letter that doesn't get published because it wasn't timely or local enough, there are 10 more that didn't get published because they never actually made it to the editor. And I know that it is scary and anxiety-provoking, and I get that little rush of butterflies as my finger sort of hovers over that send button every time I do this. But I always try to remind myself that submitting my letter, I can't guarantee that it will get published. But if I don't submit that letter, I can guarantee that it So here on staff, we are here to support you in this. You'll see on the next slide here, a whole slew of tools, templates, how-to guides linked to the local data on Snap. more info on the Farm Bill, you know, we've got all those policy specifics if you're looking for them in a handful of other things there too. You know, if you want to go on to the next step and work on an op-ed or an editorial, that's fantastic, but also don't forget these decisions are being made now so time is important. So don't get lost in a draft that never gets submitted. And when you do get published, be sure to send it directly to your members of Congress reminding them of your ask and also send it to us here at the office so that we can help make sure even more folks see it. So, you know, later tonight or tomorrow when you sit down and you're staring your notebook or your computer screen, figuring out what you want to write or Better yet, you get to that moment when you're ready to finally hit submit. You get that sort of rushing anxiety and nerves. Remember that you are not in this alone. I think some of the real power of this month's action is that we are getting sent on these together. And that if all of us can hit submit today, tomorrow, the next day, I know that by Friday and Saturday and Sunday, those members of Congress are going to be seeing your letters showing up in their Google alerts. If you manage to get your letter published before they come back to Washington, you may even have the luck of having your letter printed in the hard copy of the paper that gets dropped on their front step and they can walk out and pick it up and read it themselves at their kitchen table. So think about that as opportunity and motivation to get your letter in there so they can hear from us why this matters and what we want them to do about it. So happy writing everybody. With that I think I'll turn it back over to Joss.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_07",
