  {
    "episode_id": 75093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "Thanks, Bill. And thanks, everybody. Thanks, Joss, for inviting us. So we're really pretty excited about this presentation format that we've been developing this spring. So far, as Bill said, we have a 45-minute and an hour and 15-minute version. And as we do when we lobby Congress, each of us take a different part. So team effort. We open with an introduction to results, either short or more complete depending on time. The longer one adds an overview of a successful campaign and all the steps it takes to get a congressman on board. Then we present the results laser speech, explain the epic format and how to use it for powerful speaking and writing. We follow that with a five minute PowerPoint giving background on the issue at hand. In this case, it was snap this spring. We found that 12 slides is plenty of information for five minutes. Then we introduce the results method of learning laser speech and using the one on SNAP this time. We take the group through it three times, having people read it aloud, then do shout outs, give the group practice and raising their voices and learning the facts. Finally, we asked them to take the action on the spot. It's pretty shocking for some of them. We asked the group leader to model making the call, which seemed to break the ice. And we projected slides, the member of Congress, phone numbers, and the congressional switchboard number, and then walked the room as they made the calls, encouraging them on and answering questions. And we've finished with ways to follow up, join the action list, come to a meeting, or even come to a face-to-face, and give information about real change and the IC. And we can't wait to try it again. We find it's really effective, and it's bringing energy and vitality to results in our area. I'm glad to share anybody wants more information.",
