  {
    "episode_id": 75093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 79,
    "turn_text": "Well, I call this little presentation, stalking your senator, because that's how I feel I've been working on this. And interesting what my motivation was. About six months ago, I called the Ag Aid of Senator Ernst, who's one of our senators, and had a great talk with him. And he said, oh, call back any time. So I did call back. And just last month, and I called three times, and I left messages, e-mailed. I was getting nowhere. So we set up a meeting with our regional, or the senator's regional director and ag aide, and had a great meeting with them. But at the end of the meeting, I sort of told them, the Washington people, because it really irked me. I am beginning to understand that they are working for me. And that is what made me angry because I thought, how dare you ignore me. So they immediately dialed Washington DC. And by golly, I had a message back. And so I met or I phoned them and set up a phone conversation with the Ag Aid in Washington, D.C. But in the meantime, Senator Ernst was having a town hall about 45 minutes away. And I tell you, 45 minutes of Iowa in April, wet and muddy was not a circle to drive it. But anyway, so which is there. And surprisingly, she introduced me to the audience that was there. She said, Judy works with results and she, you know, pays attention to people who are struggling in our state. And so I talked with her, got a sort of a different take on the SNAP than I got from the regional aid. And then, and a real interesting one which I'll go back to. But anyway, so then I went home and then I phoned the Ag Aid in Washington DC and talked with him about work requirements. And then I wrote a letter to the editor a couple days ago and I don't know if he'll get published because there was a gorgeous letter to the editor about SNAP today. So maybe that will take the place of mine. And then I found some information out about SNAP and military families. And I thought it was interesting information for our senator because she's a real military person. So I sent that to both of her aides and got a thank you from the one in Washington, D.C. Thank you for keeping us informed and so on. Here's Senator Ernst's take on SNAP. she really doesn't mind the work requirements. They don't bother her. However, she would ditch them for the sake of bipartisanship. So bipartisanship is really important to her on this issue. And I do wonder how many other senators are like that. So we can hope the Democrats very loud voice in that committee because they are the ones who are asking for no cuts to the program. So that's my stalking story. Thank you.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
